  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nTemperatures have been running a few degrees above the NBM\nforecast the last few days, so would expect quite a few middle\n70 readings this afternoon and Saturday. Records for today are \nin the 75 to 80 range, which may be just out of reach. \nSaturday's record of high of 71 at Cape Girardeau will \ndefinitely be in play.\n\nThe ensemble guidance indicates there is now a near certainty of\nsome measurable light rain over the entire Quad State Sunday,\nmainly in the afternoon into the evening. They also indicate a \n50-70% chance of over a tenth of an inch. The 00Z EC and GFS \nbring around 200J/kg of MUCAPE near to southeast Missouri, so a \nrumble of thunder is not completely out of the question in \nsoutheast Missouri, and some lucky places that get a heavier \nshower could see closer to a quarter inch of rainfall. It is \nquite sad that receiving a quarter inch of rainfall would be \nconsidered lucky. This isn't California!\n\nThe cold front will gradually bring cooler air to the region,\nwith below normal high temperatures expected through all of next\nweek. The coolest air currently is forecast for Wednesday when\nhighs will be in the mid to upper 40s or around 10 degrees below\nnormal throughout the region. Winds will remain out of the north\nthrough the week.\n\nAs for precipitation chances, the medium range guidance is still\nstruggling to settle on a path for a storm system in the Tuesday\nthrough Thanksgiving Day time-frame. There has been a \nconsistent signal for upper-level energy to dive southeast \nthrough the Plains and then move east along baroclinic zone \nlocated along the northern Gulf Coast. The best guess at this \ntime, is that the storm system and associated precipitation will\nremain to the south of the Quad State. However, WPC continues \nto bring a 20% chance of light rain mainly into west Kentucky, \nlate Tuesday night into Wednesday. We will keep a close eye on \nthe forecast trend on this potential storm system.",
